==English==
{{was wotd|2009|January|11}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ML.|en}} {{m|la|āctuātus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|āctuō||actuate, implement}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|āctus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|agō||do, act}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈæktʃu.eɪt/|/ˈæktju.eɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈæktʃu.eɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-actuate.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-au-actuate.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|actuat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[activate]], or to put into [[motion]]; to [[animate]].
#* Johnson
#*: Wings, which others were contriving to '''actuate''' by the perpetual motion.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[incite]] to [[action]]; to [[motivate]].
#* '''1748'''. HUME, David ''Enquiries concerning the human understanding and concerning the principles of moral.'' 2. ed. London: Oxford University Press, 1973. § 11.
#*: A man in a fit of anger, is '''actuated''' in a very different manner from one who only thinks of that emotion.
#* Addison
#*: Men of the greatest abilities are most fired with ambition; and, on the contrary, mean and narrow minds are the least '''actuated''' by it.

